 ### Amplitude, phase and coverage
 
-To separate the semidiurnal contributions, we group M₂ with the larger lunar elliptic semidiurnal tide, N₂, and the principal solar semidiurnal tide, S₂, with the lunisolar semidiurnal tide, K₂. Within each group, relative amplitudes and phases follow the physical model. The primary reduction, with no empirical daily terms in the fit, gives lunar-group and near-12-h-group responses of $0.97\pm0.14$ and $0.90\pm0.29$ (Fig. 2 and Extended Data Fig. 1). We find an auxiliary full-template response of $0.96\pm0.11$, where a response of one denotes agreement with the predicted change in frequency ratio (Fig. 3a). The auxiliary reduction gives a lunar-group response of $1.00\pm0.13$ (Fig. 3a). Furthermore, a free-phase lunar fit gives an amplitude of $0.98\pm0.11$ and a phase offset of $-6°\pm8°$ (segment-bootstrap uncertainties), and the auxiliary phase fit likewise agrees with the predicted timing (Fig. 3b).
