 ### Separate semidiurnal detections
 
-As shown in Fig. 3c, the equivalent-height amplitude spectrum has lunar and solar-frequency peaks of 4.87 and 2.87 cm at 22.361 and 23.148 μHz. Subtracting the fixed unit-amplitude physical prediction reduces the amplitudes at these frequencies to 0.71 and 1.02 cm. Each principal frequency retains its peak when the other is controlled by a freely phased sinusoid, with N₂ and K₂ also controlled (Extended Data Fig. 4). A joint fit gives M₂ and S₂ amplitudes of 4.13 and 1.76 cm ($4.50\times10^{-18}$ and $1.92\times10^{-18}$; Supplementary Table 1); these differ from the scan peaks because neighbouring frequencies are fitted simultaneously. The conditional spectra thus separate the M₂ and S₂-frequency contributions without imposing their astronomical phase relation. Detection of the near-12-h contribution is quantified by the predicted-phase (S₂, K₂) group response of $0.90\pm0.29$ (Extended Data Fig. 1).
