 ### Reliability of the separated signals
 
-Reciprocal subtraction tests the coupling introduced by the sampling (Extended Data Fig. 5). Removing the jointly fitted lunar waveform changes the solar-frequency amplitude from 2.67 to 1.76 cm ($2.918$ to $1.921\times10^{-18}$), and removing the solar-frequency waveform changes the lunar amplitude from 4.45 to 4.13 cm ($4.857$ to $4.505\times10^{-18}$); both remain identifiable. Moreover, the maximum canonical correlation between the projected sine–cosine pairs is 0.290 (Extended Data Fig. 6), and the strongest sampling sidelobes respond to lunar-waveform subtraction as predicted (Supplementary Fig. 2).
